OK, I've hit a wall on finding the actual SDK docs beyond the hooks page you already shared. Here's what I can piece together:

## What the Copilot SDK appears to be

Based on the [working-with-hooks doc](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/copilot-sdk/use-copilot-sdk/working-with-hooks) and the SDK overview page:

It's a **library** (available in Node.js, Python, Go, .NET, Java) that lets you build your own agent **on top of GitHub Copilot's model backend**. You create a `CopilotClient`, register hooks, define tools, and manage sessions programmatically. The `onUserPromptSubmitted` hook can return a `modifiedPrompt` — meaning you intercept every user message and can enrich it with memory before it hits the model.

## What that means for Pallium

The integration would look like:

```typescript
const session = await client.createSession({
    hooks: {
        onSessionStart: async () => ({
            additionalContext: await palliumQuery("recent decisions, progress")
        }),
        onUserPromptSubmitted: async (input) => {
            const memories = await palliumQueryAndIngest(input.prompt);
            return { modifiedPrompt: `${memories}\n\n${input.prompt}` };
        },
    },
});
```

This gives you full per-turn injection parity with Claude Code.

## The catch

You'd be building a **separate agent application** that uses GitHub Copilot's LLM as its backend. This is NOT configuring VS Code Copilot — it's building a standalone agent that happens to use Copilot's API. Users would interact with it through whatever UI you build or through a Copilot Extension, not through the normal VS Code Copilot Chat.

So it's basically: build your own agent (like Pelican is for Claude), but powered by Copilot's model access and SDK conveniences.

## My honest take

This isn't really a "Copilot integration" — it's "build an independent agent using GitHub's LLM API." At that point you could equally use the OpenAI API, Anthropic API directly, or any other provider. The SDK just gives you GitHub's model access + some session management.

The question becomes: is there value in Pallium supporting a generic agent SDK (not tied to a specific IDE integration), or is the goal specifically to make Copilot-in-VS-Code have memory? If the latter, you're stuck waiting for `UserPromptSubmit` to get `additionalContext` support in the VS Code hooks system.

see this review by codex: 
**Findings**

::code-comment{title="[P1] Stdio MCP server is configured without a Pallium base URL" body="The proposed `.mcp.json` only sets `PALLIUM_MCP_TRANSPORT=stdio`, but `app/mcp/context.py` treats the server as unconfigured unless `PALLIUM_BASE_URL` is set. As written, every MCP tool returns the not-configured message, so agent-initiated memory access is dead on arrival. Add `PALLIUM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:19836` for stdio, or use Codex's HTTP MCP config against `http://localhost:19836/mcp`." file="C:/Dev/rore/Pallium/docs/specs/2026-05-03-codex-integration-design.md" start=218 end=229 priority=1 confidence=0.97}

::code-comment{title="[P1] Global memory instructions contradict the shipped visibility model" body="The spec tells Codex to store cross-project/global memories with `visibility: public` and no `container_ref`, but the current privacy model says ingest without `container_ref` is not promoted for the scope-aware package, while cross-container/shared memory is still roadmap-later work. This creates a user-visible promise that explicit global remembers will work when the backend explicitly fails closed. Either remove this from the Codex instructions or make it an integration-scoped feature with roadmap/backend changes." file="C:/Dev/rore/Pallium/docs/specs/2026-05-03-codex-integration-design.md" start=280 end=285 priority=1 confidence=0.95}

::code-comment{title="[P2] Mandatory rate/evidence calls turn the agent into a noisy memory workflow" body="The AGENTS block requires rating every injected block and fetching evidence for every relevant `[+source]` card before responding. That conflicts with the thin-client contract in `docs/agent-integration.md`, where Pallium owns injectability and evidence is fetched only when the card is insufficient. This will add tool latency on routine turns, increase failure modes, and encourage agents to treat memory injection as a second workflow rather than trusted context." file="C:/Dev/rore/Pallium/docs/specs/2026-05-03-codex-integration-design.md" start=258 end=270 priority=2 confidence=0.9}

::code-comment{title="[P2] Plugin packaging is treated as equivalent to global setup before hook discovery is proven" body="The manifest and alternative plugin installation path imply plugin-local hooks and MCP config are enough for distribution. Local Codex plugin examples here expose skills/interfaces, while the active Codex hook config lives in `~/.codex/hooks.json`; OpenAI Codex issue #16430 also reports plugin-local hooks not executing in current CLI versions. Keep the setup CLI as the primary path and mark plugin hooks as an explicit validation gate before presenting plugin install as equivalent." file="C:/Dev/rore/Pallium/docs/specs/2026-05-03-codex-integration-design.md" start=66 end=68 priority=2 confidence=0.78}

**Secondary Notes**

Use `sys.executable` or an installed absolute interpreter path in setup-generated hooks instead of `python3`; this repo is being reviewed on Windows, and the existing Claude setup already avoids that portability trap.

Roadmap/docs drift to call out before implementation: global/cross-container memory is still later work in `roadmap/board.md`, while this spec exposes it as an AGENTS.md behavior. The rest of the design is broadly aligned with Palliumâ€™s client-side integration model once those scope and Codex-packaging assumptions are tightened.

Sources checked: local repo docs/code plus [OpenAI Codex issue #16430](https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/16430) and related hook behavior reports in `openai/codex`. No files were edited.
